#	nix, as vendored

This is the NIX kernel from https://shithub.us/rminnich/nix, taken at
commit f0927138 of 9 September 2026 and laid out the way the other
kernels under ../ are: jmk's 9k, changed by rminnich, with 2 MiB pages
and the NIX application cores.  Upstream keeps it at sys/src/nix in a
Plan 9 tree; here it is a flavour of its own and names nothing outside
this directory but the host toolchain in ../../utils, the top-level
../../include, and libkern, libsec and libmp, which are built from one
source tree for every target.

#	what is inside

k8		the amd64 machine, under upstream's name for it
port		the portable kernel
ip		the Internet protocol stack
386		the drivers upstream shares with its 386 kernel and k8
		takes through the "dir" section of a configuration
boot		the boot programs; see below
mk		parse, the awk program that turns a configuration into
		mk rules and into the device, system call and error
		tables, and the mkfiles it includes
root		the sources of the user programs the k8cpufs
		configuration embeds
lib		the part of the nix user libc and libip the kernel
		links, taken from upstream's sys/src/libc and
		sys/src/libip

Headers that belong to the target rather than to the source live in
the target, as for the other flavours: ../../nix/include is upstream's
sys/include with sys/src/nix/include laid over it, and
../../nix/amd64/include is upstream's amd64/include.  The system call
numbers, upstream's sys/src/libc/9syscall/sys.h, are port/sys.h.

#	building

	PATH="$PWD/MacOSX/arm64/bin:$PATH"
	cd os/nix/k8
	mk install

puts 9k8cpufs, a Plan 9 a.out for acid and inm, and 9k8cpufs.elf, the
same kernel in the ELF32 wrapper a multiboot loader reads, in
../../nix/amd64.  The library is built on the way, into
../../nix/amd64/lib/libnix.a, and libkern, libsec and libmp are
expected in ../../Inferno/amd64/lib:

	for i in libkern libsec libmp; do
		(cd $i && mk SYSTARG=Inferno OBJTYPE=amd64 install)
	done

k8cpufs is the configuration that is built: a stand-alone cpu server
with rc and a few programs embedded as /boot.  It is what upstream
boots, with

	qemu-system-x86_64 -machine pc,acpi=off,hpet=off -cpu Opteron_G3 \
		-smp 4,sockets=4,cores=1,threads=1 \
		-netdev user,id=net0,hostfwd=tcp::2323-:23 \
		-device e1000,netdev=net0 \
		-m 8192M -kernel 9k8cpufs.elf -nographic

and reaches rc's prompt on the serial console with all four cores up.

#	what is built here and what is not

Everything in the kernel proper is compiled and linked by the tree's
own 6c, 6a and 6l.  Two things are inputs taken from upstream, because
they are user programs and this tree does not build the nix user
libc they link against:

k8/init.out	the first user process, init9.c and port/initcode.c
		linked against upstream's libc.a; init.h is derived
		from it here
k8/k8cpufs.root.s
		the /boot programs the configuration embeds -- rc, ls,
		factotum, ipconfig, listen1, cpu, execac and the
		rest -- as upstream's mkrootall wrote them

The mk rules that would regenerate them are not run: the "rootdir"
section of the configuration is read for the device table only, and
mk/parse is not asked for its root rules.  For the same reason the
boot programs in boot/ and the k8cpu and k8root configurations, which
need one, are not built, and neither are libseg, libtube and test,
which are user-side.  port/latin1.h is upstream's generated file;
upstream derives it from /lib/keyboard with aux/mklatinkbd.

#	what was changed from upstream

k8/syscall.c, port/syscallfmt.c and the generated port/systab.c read
../port/sys.h rather than /sys/src/libc/9syscall/sys.h.

port/print.c defines errfmt, so that %r works: libkern names it in its
verb table, and upstream's is in libc, built on the rerrstr system
call.  It is the 9front kernel's definition.

mk/parse: its regular-expression rules are written the way mk reads
them on this host -- the target anchored with '$' and the prerequisite
quoted, so the \1 survives; the -D_DBGC_ flag in the sh spelling, since
mk here runs recipes with sh; the path of sys.h and objtype taken from
-v.  mk/portmkfile and k8/mkfile are rewritten against
../../../mkconfig and ../../../mkfiles/mkfile-nix-amd64, with od where
upstream used xd and the tree's ndate and ksize.  The "lib" section of
a configuration is not consulted; the libraries are named in k8/mkfile.

The ELF is linked with -P0x110000, the physical address of the text,
which the tree's loader takes explicitly where upstream's 6l masked it
out of every address.
